(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-09":{"number":9,"title":"A Bad Day in G Major","content":"The bad day began with a cup of coffee I did not want and ended with Greg turning off every light in the room.\n\nIn between, there were too many messages, too many sounds, and one conversation that left my skin feeling too tight.\n\nI woke already tired. The bakery below was louder than usual. The delivery truck arrived before dawn, its brakes shrieking against the street. Someone dropped a metal tray. A child cried in the hallway. My phone lit up with notifications from three different people, each needing an answer that could not be reduced to yes or no.\n\nBy noon, I had not eaten.\n\nBy two, I had forgotten to drink water.\n\nBy four, Greg texted: I HAVE ACQUIRED A PIE. SHOULD I BRING IT TO YOU OR SHOULD I STAND OUTSIDE AND ANNOUNCE ITS ARRIVAL?\n\nI stared at the message. The idea of company made my chest clench. The idea of being alone made it worse.\n\nI typed: I DON’T KNOW.\n\nHis reply came immediately.\n\nTHAT’S OKAY. CAN I COME TO THE BUILDING AND WAIT IN THE HALL? YOU DON’T HAVE TO LET ME IN.\n\nI looked at the door. “Okay,” I said aloud, though he could not hear me.\n\nA few minutes later, he knocked once.\n\nI opened the door only a crack. Greg stood in the hallway with a pie box balanced on one palm. He had not brought his guitar. He had not brought the chair. He had only the pie and a thermos.\n\n“Hi,” he said softly.\n\n“Hi.”\n\n“I’m here.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Would you like me to come in?”\n\nI wanted to say yes. I also wanted to disappear under the bed.\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“Okay.” He set the pie box on the floor. “Can I sit outside?”\n\nI nodded.\n\nGreg sat with his back against the opposite wall. I closed the door but did not lock it. The hallway remained between us, a narrow strip of carpet and yellow light.\n\nFor several minutes, neither of us spoke.\n\nThe silence did not fix the day. It made the day survivable.\n\n“There was a call,” I said through the door.\n\n“You don’t have to tell me.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You can tell me if you want.”\n\nI sat on the floor with my back against the door. The wood was cool against my shoulder.\n\n“They said I was selfish,” I said. “They said I should be grateful. They said I always make things complicated.”\n\nGreg was quiet.\n\n“I keep hearing it,” I continued. “Even after the call ends. It’s like they left the whole conversation in my head.”\n\n“That sounds awful.”\n\n“I can’t make it stop.”\n\n“Do you want advice or company?”\n\nThe question made my eyes burn.\n\n“Company.”\n\n“Okay.”\n\nHe stayed.\n\nThe pie sat between us on the other side of the door. The smell of apples and cinnamon slipped beneath it.\n\n“You can eat later,” Greg said. “Or not eat pie. I’m not the pie police.”\n\n“You would be a terrible cop.”\n\n“I have no authority and I’m easily bribed.”\n\nMy breathing hitched into a laugh. It did not last, but it was enough.\n\nThe phone buzzed again. I flinched so hard my shoulder struck the door.\n\n“Do you want me to answer it?” Greg asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Do you want me to hold it?”\n\nI unlocked the door and held the phone through the opening. Greg took it without looking at the screen. He placed it face-down inside my apartment.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“You’re welcome.”\n\n“I hate this.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I hate needing people.”\n\n“I know that too.”\n\nThe words came out sharper than I intended. “You don’t know everything.”\n\nGreg did not defend himself. He nodded through the gap in the door.\n\n“You’re right,” he said. “I don’t.”\n\nThe lack of argument took the fight out of me.\n\n“I don’t want you to see me like this,” I whispered.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I’m not charming.”\n\n“I’m not here for charming.”\n\n“You like me when I’m funny.”\n\n“I like you when you’re funny because you’re funny. I like you when you’re quiet because you’re quiet. I like you when you’re angry because your anger tells me something matters.”\n\n“What about when I’m a mess?”\n\n“You’re a person.”\n\nThe answer was so plain that I could not argue with it.\n\nGreg reached toward the door. His fingers rested against the wood, not trying to open it.\n\n“Can I hold your hand?” he asked.\n\nI slid my hand beneath the gap. His fingers met mine. The contact was light at first. When I curled my hand around his, he held on.\n\nThe hallway lights were too bright. I closed my eyes.\n\n“Tell me five things you can hear,” Greg said.\n\n“Are you giving me advice?”\n\n“I’m asking a question.”\n\nI listened.\n\n“The bakery mixer. The pipes. Someone walking upstairs. The refrigerator. Your breathing.”\n\n“Good. Four things you can feel.”\n\n“The floor. The door. Your hand. My sleeve.”\n\n“Three things you can smell.”\n\n“Cinnamon. Dust. Your coffee.”\n\n“Two things you can see.”\n\nI opened my eyes. Through the narrow gap, I could see his shoe and the edge of the pie box.\n\n“Your shoe. The pie.”\n\n“One thing you know.”\n\nI looked at his hand.\n\n“You’re here,” I said.\n\nGreg squeezed my fingers. “I am.”\n\nThe room slowly returned to its proper size.\n\nAfter a while, I opened the door wider. Greg entered only when I moved aside. He put the pie on the counter and waited.\n\n“Lights?” he asked.\n\n“Off.”\n\nHe turned them off one at a time. The studio became dark except for the gray light from the window.\n\n“Sound?”\n\n“Low.”\n\nGreg silenced his phone and set it beside mine.\n\nThe screen lit up once more before it went dark. I saw Steven's name and felt a fresh pulse of guilt.\n\n\"Was that your kid?\"\n\n\"Yeah.\" Greg looked at the message preview. \"He's wondering if I found the pie place. He knows I went looking.\"\n\n\"You can answer.\"\n\n\"Could I send one text, then put it away? I'd like him to know I'm not stranded in the middle of a pie-related emergency.\"\n\nI laughed, even with my shoulders still pressed up near my ears.\n\nHe sent a short reply: FOUND PIE. FRIEND HAVING QUIET DAY. TALK TOMORROW. Then he turned the phone over and left it on the counter.\n\n\"You didn't have to tell him about me.\"\n\n\"Didn't tell him anything private. Just that I'm occupied tonight. You get to have your own story.\"\n\nI looked at the box on the counter. Its cardboard had softened on one corner where the rain got in, and the smell of cinnamon was almost too much in the small room.\n\n\"Could you put the pie in the kitchen? The smell's making me feel a little sick.\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\" He picked it up and stopped. \"Do you want me to open the window?\"\n\n\"Not yet. Street's loud.\"\n\n\"Right.\" He crossed to the kitchen and came back without trying to coax me into eating.\n\nThe room wasn't magically peaceful. Pipes knocked somewhere behind the wall. A car horn sounded outside. But there was room now to decide which noise I could bear, and the man beside me didn't mistake every adjustment for criticism.\n\n“Distance?”\n\nI looked at the couch. “Close.”\n\nHe sat beside me, leaving a careful inch between my thighs.\n\n“Touch?”\n\nI took his hand. “Yes.”\n\nHe kissed my knuckles. There was no hunger in the gesture, only a reminder that I was in my body and my body belonged to me.\n\nI ate a small piece of pie. Greg ate a larger one and claimed the difference was medically necessary.\n\n“You’re not fixing me,” I said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You’re not even trying.”\n\n“I don’t think you’re broken.”\n\nI stared at him in the dimness.\n\n“I think you’re having a terrible day,” he continued. “I think terrible days are allowed to be terrible. I think I can sit next to you while one happens.”\n\nMy throat closed.\n\n“You’re allowed to leave,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I might be too much.”\n\n“You might be more than I can carry on a particular day.”\n\nThe honesty hurt less than a reassurance would have.\n\n“But,” he added, “that doesn’t make you too much. It makes us two people with limits.”\n\nI leaned my head against his shoulder. Greg stayed still until I settled.\n\n“What are your limits?” I asked.\n\n“I need people to tell me when they’re angry instead of turning silence into a maze. I need to sleep more. I need to stop pretending coffee is a personality.”\n\n“That last one is impossible.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nWe remained together until the sky darkened completely. Greg told me a story about the first time Steven tried to cook and somehow made a cake sing. I told him nothing for a long while, then told him the small truth that I was beginning to believe this town might be somewhere I could stay.\n\n“That’s a big thing to say on a bad day,” he murmured.\n\n“I’m trying not to wait for good days to say important things.”\n\nHe turned his face toward mine. “Can I kiss you?”\n\nI nodded.\n\nThe kiss was slow and quiet. When I pulled back, Greg rested his forehead against mine.\n\n“Still here?” he asked.\n\n“Still here.”\n\nThe bad day did not disappear. But it stopped being the only thing in the room.\n\nGreg stayed the night, but he slept on top of the blankets until I asked him to come closer. In the morning, he made toast and left a glass of water beside my bed before saying anything about breakfast.\n\n“You’re learning,” I said.\n\n“I’m trying to become less of a golden retriever with a guitar.”\n\n“You can keep the guitar.”\n\n“That’s a relief.”\n\nI sat at the table while he buttered the toast. The light was gentler than it had been the day before. My phone remained face-down, but it no longer seemed capable of ruling the room.\n\n“I want to make a plan for bad days,” I said.\n\nGreg put the knife down. “Okay.”\n\n“Not a plan where you become responsible for managing me.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“A plan where I can tell you what helps without having to explain it while I’m already overwhelmed.”\n\n“We can write it down.”\n\nI found a notepad. Together, I made a list. Low lights. Quiet voices. Ask before touching. Water within reach. No surprise visitors. Company without questions unless I ask for questions. Food available, but no pressure to eat. Reminders that I am not in trouble.\n\nGreg added one line beneath the others: DO NOT TURN CARE INTO A DEBT.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“That one is for me too,” he said.\n\nI wrote another: WE CAN CHANGE THE PLAN.\n\nHe underlined it twice.\n\nWhen the list was finished, Greg took a picture and put it in his phone. I folded the paper into my wallet.\n\n“This doesn’t mean I’ll always get it right,” he said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“It means I’ll keep trying to get it right without making you comfort me for trying.”\n\nI reached across the table. He took my hand.\n\n“You’re allowed to have bad days too,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“And you’re allowed to tell me when you don’t have the capacity.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You’re not allowed to turn into a martyr.”\n\nGreg sighed. “You have taken away all my hobbies.”\n\n“You still have pancakes.”\n\n“They are not enough.”\n\nI smiled. The day was not perfect, but it had a map now.","word_count":1914},"su-24":{"number":24,"title":"A Little More Honest","content":"Greg arrived for the clinic appointment carrying an umbrella with one bent spoke and the expression of a man about to audition for a comedy show.\n\nThe waiting room smelled of floor cleaner and damp coats. Someone had taped a hand-drawn sign to the drinking fountain asking people not to leave cups in the sink. Through the window I could see Beach City traffic moving slowly around a puddle.\n\n\"Great day to get rained on by a medical professional,\" Greg said.\n\n\"We're indoors.\"\n\n\"Details.\"\n\nHe had made the appointment three times and canceled twice. Today he had asked me to accompany him, which was different from asking me to manage it.\n\n\"Do you want me at the desk with you?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. I can do that.\"\n\nHe could. He gave the receptionist his information, checked the appointment time, and came back with a clipboard that looked strangely heavy in his hands.\n\n\"Everything okay?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yeah. Just a lot of little boxes.\"\n\nI glanced at the form.\n\n\"Would you like help reading any of it?\"\n\nHe shook his head, then nodded.\n\n\"Maybe the one about emergency contacts.\"\n\n\"That's yours to choose.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"I know. I just keep thinking whoever I write down gets assigned a whole job without being asked.\"\n\n\"Then ask them before you decide.\"\n\nGreg sat beside me. He turned the form over and tried to call Steven, then stopped.\n\n\"He's got a lot going on.\"\n\n\"He can answer whether he wants to be listed. It's not the same thing as being on call every minute.\"\n\nHe made the call. Steven agreed, and Greg wrote his name with care. He asked whether I wanted to be an additional contact for visits when I was in Beach City. I told him yes, with the understanding that my travel through Lanternweald meant there would be times I wasn't reachable.\n\n\"Good,\" he said. \"Two people, two actual choices.\"\n\nThe receptionist called his name.\n\nAt the doorway, Greg turned back.\n\n\"You'll still be here?\"\n\n\"I'll be here when the appointment ends unless you tell me you'd rather go alone.\"\n\nHe smiled nervously and went inside.\n\nI did not follow. The waiting room held a rack of old magazines and a potted plant whose leaves looked as tired as I felt. I watched an older man return a library book to the woman beside him and apologize for bending the cover. She shrugged and said she was more interested in whether he'd enjoyed it.\n\nThat seemed sensible.\n\nWhen Greg came out, he was holding a card with a follow-up date.\n\n\"No apocalypse,\" he announced.\n\n\"That was never on the schedule.\"\n\n\"Then I'd like a refund for the suspense.\"\n\nThere was a shallow puddle across the direct path to the parking lot. Greg stepped toward it without looking, then caught himself when a woman using a walking stick asked the receptionist whether the side exit had a dry route.\n\nThe receptionist checked the weather notice.\n\n\"The front walk is safer today. The side path is being repaired.\"\n\nGreg moved back to make room rather than insisting he could help her across the water. I waited beside him. The woman thanked the receptionist and made her own way out.\n\nAfterward he looked at me.\n\n\"That was awkward.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"I almost did the whole heroic thing, and she hadn't asked for a hero.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to learn.\"\n\n\"I thought I was good at helping.\"\n\n\"You are. Sometimes you're so busy being ready that you forget to ask what the actual help is.\"\n\nHe considered that as we walked to the van.\n\nI had wanted to fill every silence after the appointment with reassurance. Seeing him stop his own impulse reminded me to stop mine. We hadn't reached a verdict about his future, and a follow-up card didn't make me his guardian.\n\nIn the van he put the appointment papers into an envelope instead of leaving them on the dashboard where anyone passing could read them. He wrote the date on the outside.\n\n\"Look,\" he said. \"Organized.\"\n\n\"Don't let it go to your head.\"\n\n\"Too late.\"\n\nI laughed, and the tension finally eased.\n\nWe stepped under the awning. He looked relieved, but a little shaken.\n\n\"Do you want to tell me what they said?\" I asked.\n\n\"Some of it. Not the whole thing.\"\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\nHe told me about a routine maintenance check, a recommendation to keep the follow-up appointment, and a few things he'd need to pay attention to. He didn't give me every private detail. I didn't ask for them.\n\nThen he stared at the card.\n\n\"I nearly canceled again this morning.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"You checked your phone four times before we left.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\n\"I guess I'm not subtle.\"\n\nHe leaned against the van.\n\n\"I can show up for Steven, for a friend, for somebody who needs a ride. But when it's something for me, I get scared it'll turn out expensive or complicated or that someone will tell me I've been doing everything wrong.\"\n\n\"Did anyone tell you that today?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps the next appointment can be one thing on a calendar, not a verdict on your life.\"\n\nGreg looked at the card again.\n\n\"You're annoyingly sensible.\"\n\n\"I get that a lot.\"\n\nHe held out his hand.\n\n\"Thanks for coming without taking over.\"\n\nI took it.\n\n\"It mattered that you asked.\"\n\nWe drove to the shore and ate sandwiches inside the van while the rain cleared. Greg asked how my next visit to Moonroot was shaping up. I told him the archive had reorganized its public records so nobody needed to invent a reason to ask for a private appointment.\n\n\"Sounds like the kind of place that would love a clipboard.\"\n\n\"You have no idea.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nWe'd already talked honestly about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory. I was never going to be his only source of care, and he didn't have to be mine. Today had been a small example of what we meant: asking, answering, and not turning a favor into an invisible lifetime contract.\n\nHe reached across the console.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was sweet and interrupted by the van's windshield wipers coming on unexpectedly. Greg looked at the switch as though it had betrayed him personally.\n\n\"Terrible timing.\"\n\n\"Very you.\"\n\nWe kissed again, a little slower.\n\nOn the way home, we stopped at the grocery store for bread.\n\nGreg stood in front of the cereal shelf for a long time.\n\n\"You know,\" he said, staring at the bottom row, \"I used to buy this stuff because Steven liked the prize in the box. Not the cereal. The box.\"\n\n\"Did you tell him he could just have the prize?\"\n\n\"Are you kidding? That's how you end up with a kid who doesn't eat breakfast and a dad who eats four boxes of cereal out of spite.\"\n\nI snorted. Greg reached for one, then put it back.\n\n\"I thought about asking him to come with me today,\" he said. \"To the clinic. But I couldn't tell whether I wanted company or wanted him to handle the scary parts.\"\n\n\"You chose to ask me.\"\n\n\"Yeah. And you waited without sitting in the room making all the decisions.\"\n\nI touched his elbow. \"You still get to make them when I'm with you.\"\n\nHe gave me a lopsided smile. \"Working on remembering that.\"\n\nAt the register, the clerk asked whether we wanted a loyalty card. Greg opened his mouth to launch into a joke, then actually answered the question. We bought bread, fruit and a small box of cereal. It was an ordinary purchase. He'd paid for a routine appointment without making a joke out of the receipt, and he was tired.\n\nWhen he started the van, he took the follow-up card from his pocket and checked the address before putting it away. I wanted to kiss him again. Instead I squeezed his hand on the steering wheel for one heartbeat and let him drive.\n\n\"You know what's weird?\" he asked.\n\n\"Many things. Narrow it down.\"\n\n\"I used to think caring for myself was something I had to finish before I deserved to take care of anyone else. Then I'd avoid it because there was always somebody else to help.\"\n\nI picked up the loaf we'd come for.\n\n\"That's a very complicated way to never buy bread.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nA woman behind us asked whether the van parked near the side door was ours. A delivery was trying to get through. Greg apologized, moved the van, and returned without offering to unload the truck as penance.\n\n\"Did the world survive?\" I asked.\n\n\"Barely.\"\n\nI handed him the loaf.\n\nWe paid, left the store, and drove home. It was a small day full of ordinary choices: accept a doctor's advice, ask an actual question, move a van without turning it into a performance. None of them made a grand story on their own.\n\nTogether, they felt like the kind of life I wanted to keep making with him. At home Greg placed his follow-up card on the kitchen board, turned it so the private details faced the wall, and set his own reminder. I put the umbrellas by the door and made tea.\n\nHe sat beside me on the couch, warm and exhausted, and asked whether I would stay that evening.\n\n\"I'd like to.\"\n\nThe rest of the night remained private.\n\nThe next morning, Greg checked the date on the card and set a reminder himself. He didn't make me responsible for remembering.\n\nHe did ask whether I'd join him for breakfast.\n\nI said yes.","word_count":1600},"su-39":{"number":39,"title":"The Song We Keep","content":"Greg had written the last line of the song three times.\n\nOn the first scrap of paper, it promised a forever neither of us had asked for.\n\nOn the second, it sounded like somebody leaving before they could be left.\n\nOn the third, the line stopped halfway through.\n\nHe put all three beside his guitar and stared at them.\n\n\"I think I've invented a new kind of writer's block,\" he announced.\n\n\"What's it called?\"\n\n\"Having feelings and being annoying about them.\"\n\nI laughed, then saw he wasn't quite joking.\n\nWe were in the garage, surrounded by cables, a small recording device, and a box of things Greg had been meaning to sort for months. The air smelled of warm dust and sea salt. One wall held posters from shows I'd never attended; another held a shelf with spare strings and the battered notebook where he kept chord progressions.\n\nThe song had begun beside Starwake Pier, where water made a low note beneath the wooden boards. Greg had copied part of that rhythm into the melody, leaving a long pause after the fifth note. He liked the pause when he played alone. Every time he tried to finish the lyrics, he filled it with a promise too large to trust.\n\n\"Play the beginning again?\" I asked.\n\nHe picked up the guitar.\n\nThe opening was gentle and slightly crooked. I knew the melody well enough to hum along, though I still missed the turn in the third phrase.\n\nGreg stopped.\n\n\"That's the wrong harmony.\"\n\n\"Sorry.\"\n\n\"No, wait. Do it again.\"\n\nI hummed.\n\nHe listened, then picked out a new chord beneath it.\n\n\"Oh,\" he said.\n\n\"Good oh?\"\n\n\"Interesting oh. That's the kind that gets you in trouble.\"\n\nHe played it again. The new chord made the next note sound uncertain, almost as though the tune had reached a doorway and didn't know whether to step through.\n\n\"Keep that,\" I said.\n\nGreg set down the guitar.\n\n\"You're not just saying that to be nice?\"\n\n\"No. I actually like the way it doesn't resolve.\"\n\nHe stared at the unfinished line.\n\n\"That's the problem.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"If I finish it,\" he said, \"people might think I'm promising something about us. Like the song's a contract.\"\n\n\"Do you think that?\"\n\n\"Sometimes I worry I'll start believing it.\"\n\nHe looked embarrassed, and I knew how much it had cost him to admit it. Greg was generous with laughter. Fear was harder. He tended to pack it inside a joke and hope the music would do the rest.\n\nI sat on the overturned crate beside him.\n\n\"We can write a song without making it a binding agreement.\"\n\n\"Sure. But then I get scared that if it's not a big promise, it doesn't mean enough.\"\n\nI thought about the green notebook in my bag, the one with my living agreements and the things I was learning to ask for.\n\n\"Can I tell you something without turning it into a verse?\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\n\"I love you. I am ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory, and we've already talked about that honestly. I don't want the song to erase those choices. I don't want it to make you a consolation prize, either.\"\n\nGreg looked down at his hands.\n\n\"That's the part I'm bad at.\"\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"Believing I matter without being the only one.\"\n\nI felt my chest tighten.\n\n\"I can't prove you matter by making somebody else disappear.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"But I can tell you why you're here with me. Because I like your terrible jokes. Because you listen when I say a plan isn't working. Because you remember what I drink when I get distracted. Because you can be scared and still choose to tell me.\"\n\nHe breathed out slowly.\n\n\"That's a pretty good list.\"\n\n\"There's also the van.\"\n\n\"Ah. The real reason.\"\n\n\"The seats are surprisingly comfortable.\"\n\nHe laughed, and it eased something in the room.\n\nHe reached for my hand.\n\n\"Can I ask you for a date next week?\"\n\n\"Yes. I have an afternoon free after Moonroot.\"\n\n\"Just us?\"\n\n\"Just us. If the archive runs late, I'll send word instead of leaving you guessing.\"\n\n\"Deal.\"\n\nWe sat for a while, listening to the little recording device hum.\n\nThen I picked up the third page.\n\n\"What if the last line isn't about forever? What if it's about something we keep doing, one time at a time?\"\n\nGreg looked at me.\n\n\"Like what?\"\n\n\"Listening.\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Too much like a public service announcement.\"\n\n\"Coming back?\"\n\n\"Too much like a ferry schedule.\"\n\nI grinned.\n\n\"You're impossible.\"\n\n\"Artistically sensitive.\"\n\n\"Same thing.\"\n\nHe played the melody again. This time he left the space open, and I hummed my wrong harmony over the next chord. It didn't resolve neatly. It sounded like two people who had heard each other and still had different ways of reaching the same place.\n\nGreg's eyes lit up.\n\n\"That's it.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"We don't need another line.\"\n\n\"Seriously?\"\n\n\"The space is the line.\"\n\nI stared.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"That's either brilliant or you've avoided writing words so successfully you've declared silence a lyric.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nWe tested the song on the little recording device. The first take had a creaking chair in the middle of the pause. The second caught the sound of a truck backing up outside. On the third, Greg missed a chord because I'd made him laugh.\n\nWe kept the third.\n\nGreg listened to the playback once, then rubbed his palm over the strings to stop them ringing.\n\n\"There's a little squeak before the last pause.\"\n\n\"From your chair?\"\n\n\"From me moving my hand. Happens when you're too busy thinking about the next chord.\"\n\n\"Want another take?\"\n\nHe thought about it. \"Nah. I want to see how it sounds when we're not staring at the recorder.\"\n\nHe'd brought a little tin of citrus tea home from the Jasmine Dragon, and it sat on the garage shelf between a spare cable and a jar of guitar picks. Iroh had carefully written the brewing instructions. Greg had added a note beneath them: *Don't forget the cup.*\n\n\"Do you actually like that tea?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yeah. Different from coffee. I won't tell the coffee; it'd get jealous.\" He reached for the tin. \"Iroh talks about drying leaves like I talk about getting a chorus right. The weird part is, he's probably more fun to argue with.\"\n\n\"He'd enjoy that assessment.\"\n\n\"Good. Tell him the smoke shouldn't drown out the orange.\"\n\nI laughed. Greg poured hot water into two mismatched mugs and let the leaves steep for the time written on the lid. He didn't try to turn the blend into a metaphor for us. He just wanted something warm before we went down to the pier.\n\nWhile we drank, he put a pencil mark above the empty line in the song. It wasn't another lyric. It was a cue to breathe, to let the listener hear the last chord die away before the melody moved on.\n\n\"That's better,\" he said.\n\nI leaned in to kiss him. He tasted faintly of orange peel and laughed when the guitar slipped sideways against his knee.\n\n\"Okay,\" he said. \"Now *that's* definitely a reason to stop recording.\"\n\nIt sounded like us.\n\nLater we took the guitar down to the pier. The boards were dry, the safety notices were posted, and the wind carried the smell of the ocean through the railings. Greg checked the stage schedule with the volunteer steward before setting up. We weren't there to take over a public performance space; we wanted five minutes to hear how the new chord sounded outdoors.\n\nA young busker was already playing near the bench.\n\n\"Mind if we wait?\" Greg asked.\n\nThe musician shook their head.\n\n\"Not at all. I've got ten minutes left.\"\n\nWe listened until their set finished, then helped move the stand to storage. Greg played our song for the open air.\n\nThe last phrase hung between the sea and the wooden railing.\n\nNo triumphant resolution.\n\nNo tragic ending.\n\nA breath.\n\nI leaned against the post.\n\n\"What do you think?\" I asked.\n\nGreg looked at the page, then at me.\n\n\"I think I'm done trying to make this song answer questions it wasn't asked.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"That's a good place to stop.\"\n\nHe set the guitar in its case.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe kissed me warmly, one hand against my cheek, and pulled back with a laugh when a gust of wind blew his hair into my face.\n\n\"Very romantic.\"\n\n\"I was aiming for majestic.\"\n\n\"You hit ridiculous.\"\n\n\"Close enough.\"\n\nOn the way home, Greg stopped at the empty rehearsal hall to return a borrowed microphone stand.\n\nThe caretaker was stacking chairs. She glanced at the notebook under Greg's arm.\n\n“Finished your big song?”\n\n“Mostly,” Greg said. “I left a space at the end.”\n\n“Can't sell a space on a record.”\n\n“Good thing I'm not selling one tonight.”\n\nThe caretaker shrugged and carried on. Greg looked almost embarrassed by how much the exchange had mattered.\n\n“Old me would've rewritten the whole thing before she could ask again,” he admitted.\n\nI shifted the guitar case against my hip. “Old you might've had a completely different song to write.”\n\n“Yeah. He was kind of a mess.”\n\n“He was also a musician.”\n\nGreg looked at me, then back at the door. “I think I keep trying to apologize for him and make sure he gets a standing ovation. Both at once. Not super efficient.”\n\n“No. But it's an impressive workload.”\n\nHe laughed. We walked the rest of the way slowly. The boardwalk smelled of fried dough and salt; someone had left a paper cup beneath the bench. Greg stopped to pick it up and put it in the bin without making it a gesture worth announcing.\n\nAt the van, he asked whether I wanted to hear the last verse again. I did. He played it quietly, and the unfinished space arrived where it belonged. We listened to the sea move beneath the melody.\n\n“I used to think every song needed to explain why somebody stayed,” he said.\n\n“Does this one?”\n\nHe shook his head. “I think it just needs to remember we both got to choose.”\n\nI leaned close and kissed the corner of his mouth before he could make a joke out of the sentence. He smiled, the guitar resting against his knee, and I could feel the laughter he was holding back.\n\nIt wasn't an answer to every question. It was a song with room for the next thing that happened.\n\n\nAt home, we put the third page in the song notebook. We kept the earlier drafts, too, because they were part of how we'd reached this version. Greg wrote a small note beside the empty final line:\n\n*Leave room for the next day.*\n\nThat evening we ate dinner from paper cartons and watched the light move across the garage floor. The rest of our time stayed private; no lyric, no performance, no audience.\n\nIn the morning, Greg played the unfinished song once more.\n\nHe stopped at the open space.\n\nThis time he smiled before the next chord.\n\nIt was finished enough to keep living.","word_count":1858}},"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-09":{"number":9,"title":"Stormlight","content":"The storm came from two directions.\n\nOne was the ordinary sea, grey and furious beyond Tideglass Cove. The other blew through an unfinished Threadway, carrying rain that tasted of iron and sending sparks down the old lantern chain.\n\nBy the time the first bell rang, three houses near the shore had flooded.\n\nMaui ran toward the water with his hook raised. I ran toward the square with the plans we'd drawn for the cove's drains.\n\nWe met halfway, both breathless, both angry.\n\n“I can hold the waves,” he said, planting his hook against the wind. “I've made the ocean blink first. I can do it again.”\n\n“For how long?”\n\n“As long as I have to.”\n\n“And then what?”\n\nHe looked at the houses, and I saw the calculation behind his bravado. He could lift stones and alter the current for a while, but the opening would keep pulling more water through.\n\n“I need help,” he said.\n\nIt was not loud. The storm nearly swallowed it.\n\n“I have a plan,” I answered.\n\nThe plan stopped being a plan as soon as people began asking real questions.\n\nAt the bakery door, Mara stood with a tray of bread she hadn't been able to move before the first floodwater reached the lower street. Pip was guiding residents toward the higher path, but the lantern on the corner had gone out. Neri had a list of safe rooms and no way to know which households were still below the tide markers.\n\n\"Can we open the hall?\" I asked.\n\n\"We can,\" Neri said. \"But only if someone checks the doors and keeps the exits clear. People need to know they can leave when they choose.\"\n\n\"I'll take the western stair,\" Pip offered.\n\n\"With another person,\" Neri said. \"The boards might be wet.\"\n\nMaui listened while someone from the cove described water lifting against their kitchen step. He held his hook with both hands.\n\n\"I could pull the whole house up,\" he said.\n\n\"Not with people inside,\" Fen answered sharply. They had arrived soaked, carrying their tide notes in a waxed pouch. \"You'll split the foundation. Tell me where the current comes from.\"\n\nI spread the map over a sheltered crate. The sketch from our earlier work showed the lower traveler arch and the shallow channel that led toward the harbor. It didn't show the new flow pushing through the unfinished Threadway.\n\n\"We need the waterline at the eastern post,\" I said.\n\nFen supplied it.\n\n\"And the tide direction?\"\n\n\"Against the wind. That means the opening is feeding it.\"\n\nI marked the arrows. A boulder would divert the flow toward the gardens. The loose brass ring was vibrating out of alignment again, but the older brace had been salvaged for another repair and the replacement had never been finished.\n\nI felt a sharp flash of anger. We had known the part needed work.\n\nMaui saw my face.\n\n\"Not now,\" he said softly.\n\n\"Not now,\" I agreed.\n\nA family came up the square steps carrying two wet blankets and a sleepy child. Neri asked whether they wanted the hall or the upper route toward a Threadway. They chose the hall. Nobody demanded they explain where they would go afterward.\n\nMara set the tray down and handed out bread. \"One piece each to start. I'll bring more after I check the ovens.\"\n\nIt wasn't an inspiring speech. It was exactly what we needed.\n\nI sent Pip and another volunteer to the lantern corner with a safe replacement; Fen took the tideboard team. Only then did Maui and I return to the arch. It was frightening to realize the people we'd left behind were making decisions without us.\n\nIt was also the reason the plan could work.\n\nAt the breach, the air tasted metallic. Maui braced the fallen beam while I tested the ring's movement. The first attempt drove the current harder through the wrong side. We stopped, retreated to dry footing, and drew the new measurements in the mud on a board.\n\nWhen the beam slipped, Maui planted his feet and let out a roar that carried over the storm.\n\n\"All right, water! You've had your fun. Now it's my turn!\"\n\n\"Don't argue with an ocean,\" I shouted back.\n\n\"I've been arguing with the ocean longer than you've been alive!\"\n\nMini Maui clung to the tattooed edge of his shoulder, drawing an exaggerated picture of the snapped support. He noticed, swore, and shifted his grip before the beam could twist in his hands. The boast had not saved the joint. His strength had bought us the seconds to get out of its way.\n\nFen called for the next reading. Maui threw them a look that plainly said he would rather punch the storm. He counted the beats between surges instead.\n\n\"Go on,\" he called to me. \"Tell the hero where to hold the impossible thing.\"\n\n\"The left side. Only until Fen says stop.\"\n\n\"Terrible instructions. Very specific. Love it.\"\n\nHe braced the left side and kept watching Fen's raised hand. Even at his loudest, he hadn't forgotten whose call would keep us alive.\n\n\"Tell me the next step,\" he said.\n\n\"I don't know yet.\"\n\n\"Then we'll find it.\"\n\nFor once he didn't pretend certainty was an extra kind of strength. I showed him where the line needed to hold the ring while I seated the catch. He checked whether I could release my hand safely before we began.\n\nWe called Fen over to verify the current would not move into the houses when it changed. They studied the marks and gave us a short window between surges.\n\nFen made me repeat the measurements back. I was so cold my teeth clicked between the numbers, and the chalk I'd used to mark the waterline had turned to paste in my hand.\n\n\"You're leaving out the western gutter,\" they said.\n\n\"I can't see it from here.\"\n\n\"Exactly. Nobody can. The roof has come down over the drain.\"\n\nMaui glanced toward the crushed timbers. \"I could lift that.\"\n\n\"You could,\" Fen said. \"After Pip clears the families above it. One more thing falling into that channel and the flood goes into Mara's ovens.\"\n\nMaui opened his mouth, shut it, and went to ask Pip how many people remained. He could have carried the whole ruined roof over one shoulder. He could not know who was underneath without asking someone who'd been counting.\n\nNeri arrived with a list of households. One address had no mark beside it.\n\n\"Empty?\" I asked.\n\n\"Unknown,\" they said. \"Don't turn it into empty.\"\n\nThat word stayed with me. *Unknown.* It was not permission to assume the worst or announce everybody safe. We sent a pair of neighbors along the upper route and waited for their report before Fen let us begin.\n\nWhen Maui came back, he held his hook at his side rather than raising it for the crowd.\n\n\"Everyone's clear above the gutter,\" he said. \"Pip counted them twice.\"\n\n\"Then lift the roof by the north beam,\" Fen instructed. \"Slowly. I need to see where the water goes.\"\n\nMaui gave an extravagant bow, rain running down his nose. \"The great Maui, assistant to a very wet engineer. Put that one in the songs.\"\n\nI grinned despite the cold. Then he lifted, and Fen watched the current, and all of us stayed alive because the people who knew the route got to make the call.\n\nOnly then did we go back into the rain.\n\nWe worked side by side. Neri organized neighbors into teams to move people to higher ground. Maui shifted the largest fallen timbers out of the paths without leaving me alone at the breach. I climbed the stable side of the old bridge and called measurements back to him. Together we set the brass ring into its proper groove and secured it with a brace made from the very dock he had rebuilt for us.\n\nThe Threadway shuddered. The wind reversed. Water poured back toward the sea.\n\nFor one terrifying moment the brace began to bend.\n\nMaui braced it with his hook. I locked the final catch.\n\nThe storm on the other side went silent.\n\nWe stood in the sudden ordinary rain, shaking.\n\n“Did we do it?” I asked.\n\nMaui looked down at the ring, then at me.\n\n“We did.”\n\nNeri asked the people at the shelter not to rush back down the flooded road.\n\n\"A closed gate can be opened after a check,\" they said. \"A damaged step can fail under somebody who thinks the danger has passed.\"\n\nMara sent bread and hot water across the square. One family wanted to stay with neighbors rather than enter the hall. Neri explained which route had been checked and let them choose.\n\nFen counted the tide markers again. Pip hung a temporary warning where the lamps had gone out. Nobody assumed a lantern was safe just because its wick could still light.\n\nMaui looked at the shattered dock brace we'd used at the arch.\n\n\"I made that,\" he said.\n\n\"And you gave it up when we needed it,\" I answered.\n\nHe touched the splintered wood.\n\n\"Guess we'll build another.\"\n\n\"We'll ask what people need first.\"\n\nHe smiled despite his exhaustion.\n\n\"You're going to keep reminding me.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nPeople emerged from the workshop shelter, counting one another and checking for injuries. There was damage everywhere: mud in the gardens, broken steps, a smashed boat. Nothing had been magically made whole by saving the village.\n\nI helped Neri make a list of repairs. Maui carried the heavy pieces where they were needed. When someone began calling him the greatest hero in the valley, he shook his head.\n\n“Don't forget the people who built the plan.”\n\nHe pointed to me, then to the others who had helped.\n\nI almost protested out of habit.\n\nInstead I said, “And don't forget who moved the beams.”\n\nHe grinned, relieved.\n\nThat evening, exhausted, we sat on the dry steps of the bakery. I rested my head against his shoulder and watched the lanterns come on one by one.\n\n“I remembered something in the storm,” I said.\n\nHe waited.\n\n“I used to think the only reason anyone needed me was that I could solve things.”\n\n“That's not true.”\n\n“I know.” I swallowed. “I know it now.”\n\nHe slipped an arm around me only after I leaned closer.\n\nThe storm had left work for tomorrow. We did not have to do all of it tonight.\n\nAnd the people who would help us were already setting chairs around the square.","word_count":1746},"maui-24":{"number":24,"title":"A Place to Put Jealousy","content":"The letter was folded into the shape of a bird.\n\nIt had been left on the Lanternwell notice board with my name written in careful blue ink. Inside was an invitation to spend an afternoon at Moonroot with Iroh, reviewing tea-stained maps of the old terraces. He had drawn a tiny teacup at the bottom and, beneath it, written that the invitation was entirely optional.\n\nMaui read over my shoulder.\n\n\"A map date,\" he said.\n\n\"An archive visit.\"\n\n\"With tea.\"\n\n\"Those things can happen together.\"\n\nHe smiled. I waited for the joke. None came.\n\nWe were in Tideglass Cove a little later when he dropped the same length of rope twice. He wasn't usually clumsy with rope. That was why I noticed.\n\n\"Is your hand hurting again?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then what's wrong?\"\n\nHe pulled the line through the ring and tightened it much harder than he needed to.\n\n\"Nothing,\" he announced with the unconvincing grandeur of a man preparing to fight a rope. \"Absolutely nothing. Except that your little folded-bird invitation keeps attacking my brain, which is an impressive feat for paper.\"\n\nI put down the tide chart.\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\nHis shoulders lowered.\n\n\"Can we talk somewhere people aren't measuring knots?\"\n\nWe walked to the little shelter above the sea-glass orchard. One raised bed was sprouting; the second was still bare because Fen had refused to transplant anything until the salt reading changed. The bench Greg and Maui had built sat beside the path, facing the water. It had no plaque, though Maui had carved a tiny fish beneath one armrest.\n\nBefore we reached the bench, Maui shifted into a hawk, circled once over the orchard, and landed on the roof with a flourish of feathers. The transformation wasn't necessary. I suspected it was the nearest available substitute for pacing.\n\nWhen he became himself again, his grin was enormous and a little desperate.\n\n\"That was a spectacular entrance to a conversation I am going to handle very maturely.\"\n\n\"Is this about Iroh?\"\n\n\"Wow. You ruin all my suspense.\"\n\nMini Maui jabbed a tiny finger at his chest. He brushed the tattoo aside.\n\n\"I know what we agreed,\" Maui said. \"I know you're ethically non-monogamous, and I know you prefer polyamory. I even know I agreed to try, which was extremely noble of me.\"\n\n\"Generous of you.\"\n\n\"Legendary.\" The joke fell short. He looked at the garden instead of at me. \"I still hate imagining someone else making you laugh in a way I don't know how to.\"\n\nHe didn't turn the admission into a demand. I wanted to comfort him, but I also wanted him to understand that my other relationships weren't something we were negotiating away. We sat with the discomfort between us, and he eventually nudged my shoe with his much larger one.\n\n\"So,\" he said, \"can I be jealous and still make you dinner?\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\n\"Great. My jealous cooking is famous.\"\n\nHe stood instead of sitting.\n\n\"I know about Iroh,\" he said.\n\n\"I know you do.\"\n\n\"I know about the others. We talked about being ethically non-monogamous. You told me that you prefer polyamory, and I said I wanted to try being part of your life without treating it as a race.\"\n\n\"You did.\"\n\n\"I meant it.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"But sometimes,\" he admitted, \"I hear that you've been invited somewhere, and part of me starts inventing a story where everybody else gets the best parts of you.\"\n\nThe wind lifted a strand of hair across my face.\n\n\"Which parts?\"\n\n\"The ones that aren't tired from fixing the village. The ones that laugh because someone said something clever and not because you're trying to make me feel better.\"\n\nHis voice was almost embarrassed.\n\nI wanted to tell him he was wrong. I wanted to assure him that I laughed more with him, or loved him more, or somehow held a private scale on which I could prove he wasn't losing.\n\nAny of those answers would have made the feeling smaller at the cost of making somebody else's love a competition.\n\n\"I laugh with you because you're funny,\" I said. \"Sometimes because you're ridiculous. Those are not consolation laughs.\"\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n\"Mostly ridiculous.\"\n\n\"Frequently.\"\n\nHe sat down at last.\n\n\"What do I do with this feeling? I don't want to tell you who you may see.\"\n\n\"You can tell me when you're afraid. You can ask for time together. You can ask whether a plan has changed. But I can't promise never to love anyone else, and I won't ask them to disappear to make us feel safe.\"\n\nMaui stared at the tide.\n\n\"I don't want that either.\"\n\n\"I believe you.\"\n\nHe looked up.\n\n\"You do?\"\n\n\"Yes. Wanting reassurance isn't the same thing as wanting control. It's what you do with it that matters.\"\n\nHe leaned back.\n\n\"That's inconvenient. I was hoping there'd be a monster to fight.\"\n\n\"Jealousy doesn't have claws, Maui.\"\n\n\"Mine might.\"\n\nI laughed, and this time he laughed with me.\n\nWe sat until the breeze grew cold. I told him I could make time for a meal after my archive visit, but only if I finished when I'd said I would and didn't overpromise to Iroh. He told me he wanted us to have a day with no errands, no celebrations, and no people asking him to carry things.\n\n\"A day of doing nothing?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not nothing.\"\n\n\"What, then?\"\n\n\"Whatever we choose after breakfast.\"\n\nThat sounded wonderful.\n\nWe put it on the calendar.\n\nAt Moonroot, the map Iroh had invited me to see was spread beneath a weighted sheet of glass. A corner had been damaged by an old leak, leaving the ink blurred where the terrace boundaries met.\n\n\"This is what I wanted your opinion on,\" he said. \"It appears someone redrew a garden path after the flood. I don't know whether the new route was ever used.\"\n\nI studied the line. The nearest record showed an invoice for gravel, but no confirmation that the work had been done. Iroh didn't ask me to solve it with a flash of remembered knowledge. He asked which evidence we should look for next.\n\nNeri brought us a second document: a repair ledger with a line for stone hauling and another for the wages of three gardeners. The payments had been made a week apart.\n\n\"That might tell us where the path went,\" I said.\n\n\"Or only where someone intended it to go,\" Iroh replied.\n\nHe laid the papers side by side, leaving room between them so neither touched the damaged map. His hands were steady. It was the same careful movement he used when placing a Pai Sho tile, but there was no game to win.\n\nI asked whether the gardeners' names appeared in the archive index. Neri shook their head.\n\n\"We have their wages, not their testimony. The index describes the work as completed, but the entry was copied from the invoice. Nobody inspected the terrace afterward.\"\n\nI made a note to check the municipal survey. The discrepancy was interesting enough to make my fingers itch. For a moment I forgot how difficult Maui had found the invitation.\n\nIroh noticed me staring at the route.\n\n\"Go on,\" he said. \"Ask the question you've been holding.\"\n\n\"What if the new path was never built?\"\n\n\"Then the invoice may be a mistake, or worse. We must not choose which because it is the more satisfying story.\"\n\nI laughed a little. I knew he meant it. This was not a general issuing a plan for a city he could command. It was a man willing to leave a gap on a map until the people who had lived there could help fill it.\n\nNeri covered the papers when the archive bell rang. I could take my notes home. I could not take the original ledger or use the gardeners' names to start a public accusation. That boundary would matter on any visit, whichever person I loved happened to come with me.\n\nI thought of Maui's rope, dropped twice, and of how jealous he'd sounded without wanting to make me responsible for fixing the feeling.\n\n\"Can I tell you something without making it your problem?\" I asked.\n\n\"Certainly.\"\n\n\"Maui was afraid I would enjoy this afternoon so much that I'd stop wanting days with him.\"\n\nIroh set his cup down. \"Do you want me to reassure him?\"\n\n\"No. That's between us. I wanted you to know why I might seem distracted.\"\n\nHe nodded. \"Then we can be distracted together, briefly, and return to the map.\"\n\nWe sat in silence until I remembered the gravel invoice. Iroh smiled at the practical suggestion. It was one of the ways he differed from Maui: where Maui wanted to move the mountain, Iroh sometimes wanted to examine the date written at its foot.\n\n\"I like both of you,\" I said. \"In different ways. I've been explicit that I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I don't want either affection turned into a test the other has to pass.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps we should both resist the temptation to be the most reassuring person in the room.\"\n\nI laughed. \"You make that sound easy.\"\n\n\"Oh, it isn't. I am extraordinarily good at giving advice nobody requested.\"\n\nHe touched my hand, then waited. I leaned closer, enough to tell him the invitation was welcome. We kissed briefly beside the map table before returning to the damaged record. Neither of us claimed to have fixed jealousy or the old terrace in one afternoon.\n\nOn my way to Moonroot, I stopped at the notice board. The old departure ledger had been updated with a sign explaining how travelers could request private help leaving without surrendering their destination. Somebody had placed a bowl of chalk beside it so visitors could mark whether they'd already received assistance, then erase the mark themselves.\n\nI thought about how much had changed since everyone treated the arrival book like a list of people the vale owned.\n\nAt Moonroot, Iroh greeted me with tea and a stack of maps.\n\n\"You look thoughtful,\" he said.\n\n\"Maui and I had a difficult conversation.\"\n\n\"Would you like to tell me?\"\n\n\"Some of it.\"\n\nHe listened without asking me to make a comparison between them. We examined the old terrace drains, discovered a marking that might indicate a seasonal overflow, and argued gently over whether a particular line was a road or a watercourse.\n\nWhen I returned to Tideglass, Maui was cooking. Properly dried kindling burned beneath a pot of vegetables, and he'd set two places at the table.\n\n\"How was your date?\" he asked.\n\n\"Good. We found a possible drainage mark.\"\n\n\"Romantic.\"\n\n\"In its way.\"\n\nHe studied me.\n\n\"Did you have a good time?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHis expression softened.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nI went around the table and kissed his cheek.\n\nHe turned toward me, surprised.\n\n\"Was that a reassurance kiss?\"\n\n\"No,\" I said. \"That was a kiss because I wanted to kiss you.\"\n\n\"Important distinction.\"\n\n\"Extremely.\"\n\nHe asked if I wanted another.\n\nI did.\n\nWe burned the first piece of bread because neither of us remembered the stove. Later, with dinner rescued and the windows open to the sea, he told me he'd still felt jealous for a few minutes after I left.\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And then I went to see Fen about the ropes. I didn't make you come home early. I didn't ask Iroh to change his plans.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"That sounds like a good place to put the feeling.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Somewhere it can be heard without becoming the person in charge.\"\n\nHe considered that, then pulled me close enough that I could rest my forehead against his.\n\n\"Can I ask for something else?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"That day without errands.\"\n\n\"It's on the calendar.\"\n\n\"Can it start with sleeping late?\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\nThat night we closed the kitchen door, left the dishes soaking, and kept the remainder of our time private. The next morning the dishes were still there. So was the calendar.\n\nWe washed them together, and neither of us pretended we'd cured jealousy forever.","word_count":1999},"maui-39":{"number":39,"title":"The Keeper's Refusal","content":"Neri told us the truth without looking at either of us.\n\nThey sat in the archive's west room, where the burned ledger rested under protective cloth. The door was closed, but not locked. Severus had offered to stay and had accepted Neri's request that he wait outside.\n\nIt was the first time Neri had asked me to hear something without calling it a meeting.\n\nMaui sat beside me. He'd come because I wanted company, not because the old keepers belonged to his legend.\n\nNeri folded their hands.\n\n\"I watched someone turn a traveler away during the Closed Bells,\" they said.\n\nThe room seemed to tighten.\n\n\"How old were you?\" I asked.\n\n\"Old enough to understand the words. Young enough to believe an official would know better than I did.\"\n\nThey looked at the ledger.\n\n\"The keeper said the channels were unstable. He said opening the door might endanger everyone. A woman wanted to leave for her homeworld. She had family waiting. She begged him to let her pass.\"\n\n\"Did he?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nI heard the sea outside, impossibly distant from Moonroot Archive.\n\n\"What happened to her?\" I asked.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\nNeri's voice broke on the last word.\n\nThey took a breath.\n\n\"The keeper ordered the arch sealed. He used the language of safety and protection. He spoke as if refusing her was an act of compassion. I stood there and did nothing.\"\n\nMaui's hand tightened on his knee.\n\nI wanted to say that Neri had been a child, that they hadn't been responsible, that remembering was enough. But the woman had still been turned away. Her family had still waited on the other side.\n\n\"I don't know what you could have done then,\" I said. \"I do know what you can do now.\"\n\nNeri looked up.\n\n\"Tell the truth in the record,\" I said. \"Help us change the rules that allowed it. And don't ask the people affected to make you feel forgiven.\"\n\nThey looked as though I had struck them.\n\n\"I wasn't going to—\"\n\n\"I know,\" I said softly. \"But I think you've been carrying this alone because you wanted a version where your silence made sense.\"\n\nThey stared at the table.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe word was almost inaudible.\n\nWe stayed a while. I asked what Neri remembered about the keeper's instructions, the weather, and the public notice. I did not ask for a name Neri had already said they couldn't reliably provide. Their memory could be a witness to an action without being proof of the scratched signature in the ledger.\n\nMaui listened.\n\nAt one point Neri said, \"I thought he was trying to keep us safe.\"\n\nMaui shifted in his chair.\n\n\"I've told people I was protecting them,\" he said. \"Sometimes I was. Sometimes I was just frightened of being blamed if something went wrong.\"\n\nNeri looked at him.\n\n\"What did you do?\"\n\n\"Things I was wrong about. Spectacularly wrong. I still tried to give them a good ending.\"\n\nHe didn't turn the answer into a story about his greatness.\n\nHe closed one enormous hand around his hook.\n\n\"I hate this kind of room,\" he said. \"Usually the villain is standing right there when I make my entrance. Excellent entrance, by the way. Very memorable.\"\n\nNeri looked at him. \"What would you do if you found the keeper?\"\n\n\"Ask why he locked somebody in.\"\n\n\"And if he said he was protecting everyone?\"\n\nMaui shifted in the chair. He had faced seas taller than the square's rooftops without blinking. This question bothered him more.\n\n\"Want proof. Probably want to shout.\"\n\n\"That isn't a plan.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"It's the first five seconds of a pretty bad one.\"\n\nThey nearly smiled.\n\nOutside, the tide still had to be measured. The sealed lower passage could not be opened by the great Maui declaring himself useful. Neri had not asked for a rescue, only for a witness to listen.\n\n\"I'm excellent at making impossible things sound easy,\" Maui said. \"You don't owe anybody a more exciting version of what happened. And if they insist, send them to me.\"\n\n\"To frighten them?\" Neri asked.\n\n\"To bore them with a six-hour story about myself. Much crueler.\"\n\nNeri let out a breath that was almost a laugh. The ledger remained on the table, unchanged.\n\nLater, Severus returned and asked whether Neri wanted their account documented as a signed statement, an anonymous summary, or kept private while they considered it. They chose a signed statement with restricted access until the wording could be reviewed.\n\nOrin prepared the form.\n\nI stood beside Neri while they read it.\n\nThe final paragraph did not identify the keeper by name. It recorded what Neri had witnessed: a traveler requesting departure, an official refusing it, and a claimed safety reason that had not been documented with an alternative plan.\n\nNeri signed.\n\nThen they put down the pen and wept.\n\nI did not know whether to embrace them. I asked.\n\nThey nodded.\n\nI held them for a moment, feeling how thin their shoulders were beneath the old coat. My own throat hurt. I wanted the world to be the kind of place where admitting wrong made the consequence disappear.\n\nIt wasn't.\n\nTwo days passed before Neri asked Orin for a second copy of the testimony.\n\nWe were in Moonroot's sorting room, where scraps of damaged paper waited in shallow trays. Orin had labeled each tray by the document it came from, not by the conclusion somebody hoped it would support.\n\n\"I've been reading what I signed,\" Neri said. \"I didn't remember whether the traveler was alone.\"\n\nOrin stopped writing. \"Then we can amend the statement.\"\n\n\"Would that make the whole thing unreliable?\"\n\n\"It would make the amendment visible,\" Orin said. \"That's different.\"\n\nNeri sat down. I could see their hands shaking around the edge of the chair. Maui brought a cup of water and set it nearby without making them take it.\n\n\"I kept seeing the child's red sleeve,\" Neri said. \"The traveler might have been holding their hand. Or the child might have stood beside the official. I can't make the memory settle.\"\n\nNobody offered to tell them which version sounded more likely.\n\nOrin recorded the uncertainty in an addendum and preserved the original wording alongside it. Neri could still choose whether the statement was read publicly. The restricted copy would keep both versions, with the date of the correction and the reason for it.\n\n\"I thought remembering would make me feel less frightened,\" Neri admitted.\n\n\"Sometimes remembering gives you more than you know how to use,\" I said.\n\nMaui shifted on the other side of the table. \"I hate this part,\" he said. \"No monster to punch. No glorious entrance. Just the thing you did, staring at you until you stop trying to make it sound better.\"\n\nHe rubbed his thumb along the curve of his hook before speaking again.\n\n\"I could talk an island into calling a mistake a victory,\" he said, a crooked grin appearing. \"Doesn't change what happened. Moana was *very* good at reminding me of that. Usually while I was in the middle of my best speech.\"\n\nNeri looked at him.\n\n\"You can tell those parts?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\" His grin was quick and painful. \"Depends who's listening. The tattoos are brutal critics.\"\n\nMini Maui popped up and pointed at a dark little image farther across his chest, one Maui rarely called attention to. He covered it with his hand. I wasn't going to ask him to turn his history into entertainment for this meeting.\n\n\"The missing line matters,\" he told Neri. \"I'm good at making stories sound wonderful. Doesn't make every part true.\"\n\nIt was more honest than one of his polished lessons. His pride hadn't disappeared. Neither had his guilt. He could still be a storyteller without making the archive's evidence follow his preferred ending.\n\nNeri looked at him.\n\n\"Did you?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. And sometimes I only learned I should have listened earlier.\"\n\nThe answer made Neri breathe a little more easily.\n\nBefore we left, Orin showed us where the public copy would omit the child's details.\n\nThe public copy was shorter than I expected. Orin had removed the child's clothing, the direction of travel and anything that could be used to guess which family the child belonged to. Even the time of day was approximate.\n\n\"Is that enough to be accurate?\" I asked.\n\n\"It's accurate about the refusal,\" Orin said. \"It's not everything Neri remembered. Those are different questions.\"\n\nNeri stood beside the table, one hand on the back of a chair. They read both versions without speaking, then put a small mark beside the paragraph they were willing to have read aloud.\n\n\"Not that one,\" they said, pointing at a sentence I had thought important.\n\n\"Why?\" I asked before I could stop myself.\n\n\"Because the person who spoke it isn't here to decide.\"\n\nI apologized. Neri didn't have to forgive the question. They drew a line through the sentence, and Orin dated the amendment so the restricted testimony would still show that the text had changed.\n\nMaui watched from the doorway. He could probably have recited the entire story after hearing it once, with a booming voice and every gesture placed for effect. He was very good at that. It would have been a terrible thing to do here.\n\n\"Need me to carry the box?\" he asked Neri.\n\n\"Only as far as the archive cupboard.\"\n\n\"Then that's as far as it goes.\"\n\nMini Maui made a great show of lifting a tiny box over its head and staggering under the weight. Neri almost smiled. The lower Closed Bells route remained sealed, the keeper unnamed, and the testimony incomplete. None of those things gave us permission to make the missing pieces up. The testimony was about a forced refusal of passage. Nobody needed to turn a half-remembered child into a clue for strangers to hunt.\n\nOn the walk to the square, Maui reached for my hand. I accepted, even though I was still thinking about the scarred page and Neri's face when they said they weren't sure.\n\n\"You're allowed not to know everything,\" he said.\n\n\"So are you.\"\n\n\"Terrible rule. It spoils all my dramatic speeches.\"\n\n\"Maybe one or two of them will survive.\"\n\nHe grinned. It felt good to laugh without making the day feel smaller.\n\nAt the next council session, Neri gave permission to read the verified portion of their testimony. The room listened. A woman in the audience asked whether other witnesses could be contacted without being pressured to relive what had happened.\n\n\"Yes,\" Neri said. \"And they may refuse.\"\n\nSomeone asked whether the old keeper had meant well.\n\nI could feel the room searching for an easier ending.\n\nI stood.\n\n\"Intention matters to understanding how decisions were made,\" I said. \"It doesn't make a locked exit harmless. A person who wasn't allowed to leave was still denied a choice.\"\n\nEdda laid two diagrams on the table before the vote. One showed the old maintenance passage beneath Closed Bells, marked in red. The other showed the traveler Threadways with separate approaches and exits.\n\n“These are not the same doors,” she said.\n\nThe man beside the notice board frowned. “But people used the lower route in the flood.”\n\n“People attempted to,” Neri answered. “That is part of what my statement describes. It does not make the passage safe today.”\n\nMaui leaned forward. “Then we fix it. I can lift the stone—”\n\n“Not while people are beneath it,” Edda said.\n\nHis mouth stayed open for half a second. Mini Maui put both hands over its face.\n\n“Right,” Maui said. “Okay. No surprise demolition. Put that in the record.”\n\nI could almost hear his pride creak under the weight of choosing not to be impressive.\n\nA woman from the square asked whether Neri's uncertain memory of the child's sleeve made the entire testimony worthless.\n\n“No,” Orin said. “We have a dated record of the amendment. The uncertainty is part of the evidence. Pretending otherwise would make it less trustworthy.”\n\nThe woman considered that. She wasn't convinced, but she lowered her hand and let the next speaker be heard. Fen wanted to know who would monitor the tide before any surface trial. Lio wanted to know whether the skiff crews would be paid for the extra crossings. Mara wanted the public notices written plainly enough for somebody arriving frightened and wet.\n\nI looked at the two diagrams. The point was not to replace one heroic keeper with another. It was to build a system in which a frightened traveler could ask what was safe, hear an honest answer and still have choices.\n\nMaui slid his hook farther away from the table.\n\n“I'm available for moving stones,” he said. “After the inspectors tell me which stones. And after the people nearby agree.”\n\nNeri laughed once, surprised enough that I turned toward them. It was not forgiveness. It was a small sound in a room that had contained almost none all afternoon.\n\n\nThe council approved a review of every emergency crossing procedure. Volunteers would be trained to explain safe options and record only the information necessary to help. The lower service passage would stay closed until the structural team could inspect it; closure of an unsafe maintenance tunnel would never be used to justify blocking the independent traveler Threadways.\n\nThat distinction took an entire afternoon to write correctly.\n\nAfterward, Maui and I walked to the cove.\n\n\"I wanted to shout,\" he admitted.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You didn't.\"\n\n\"I wanted to.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Why didn't you?\"\n\n\"Because Neri wasn't asking me to make them feel good. They were trying to say something difficult. I wanted to hear it without turning their shame into my performance.\"\n\nMaui nodded.\n\nThe light had grown gold over the sea-glass orchard. I could smell wet earth and the faint spice of rosemary. The little bench stood empty.\n\nWe sat.\n\n\"I was wrong about many things,\" Maui said. \"Still am, probably.\"\n\n\"Very probably.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n\"Do you think Neri's testimony will help?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. Not because it ends the question. Because people can now ask better questions.\"\n\nHe put his hand over mine.\n\nWe watched a boat make the safe turn toward the upper landing, its lantern moving steadily through the evening light.\n\nThe harbor did not become innocent because somebody had spoken.\n\nBut the words were out in the open.\n\nThat mattered.","word_count":2395}}};for(const s of lib.series){const rec=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=rec[ch.id];if(e&&ch.chapter_number===e.number){ch.title=e.title;ch.content=e.content;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}s.total_word_count=s.chapters.reduce((n,ch)=>n+(Number(ch.word_count)||0),0);}})();
